# The whole ruleset at once over the repo the tool is dogfooded on: six rename
# scopes plus both rewrite passes.  1538 edits across 92 files apply with 0
# conflicts, //... rebuilds, and a second pass has nothing left to do.
#
# Its first run broke the rebuild with one rename (35 errors, 8 files, one
# cause): `method: UpperCamelCase` turned Engine::capabilities() into
# Capabilities(), the name of its own return type, a struct in the enclosing
# namespace.  Inside the class, and in every class derived from it, unqualified
# `Capabilities` then found the member function before the type -- GCC rejects
# the base with -Wchanges-meaning and every `Capabilities Capabilities() const
# override` with "does not name a type".  collides() only asks whether the new
# name is *taken* in the class; the missing check was whether it is *used*
# unqualified inside the class to mean an enclosing-scope entity, which the
# renamed member would hide.  That is the capture check in scan() now, and
# highway-member_snake_case hits the same check in its other form (a member
# renamed to `free` next to a static member function that calls ::free).
#
# The run also pinned a skip that was too blunt.  About twenty ABSL_FLAG names
# (docker_image, repo_dir, work_dir, cpus, ...) are spelled the same as a data
# member of the config struct they are copied into, and absl only ever pastes
# or stringizes a flag's name -- so the token at the ABSL_FLAG call site has no
# AST node at its spelling, and the spelling audit used to decline the member.
# The audit now knows which macro arguments the preprocessor consumed, and
# those members rename: 1379 edits before, 1522 after (1538 once the type and
# namespace rules joined the ruleset).
#
# NB: sourced by bash -- no backticks or dollar signs in values.
REPO=game_arena
RULESET=google_style
EXPECT=pass
